-- title:
--   Theorem 5 — sampling is a $(s\log s+\log\binom ns-s\log n+\alpha)$-approximation for MESP
-- statement:
--   Let $C=V^\top V\succeq0$ have rank $d$, where $V\in\mathbb R^{d\times n}$ has columns $v_i$, let $s\in[d]$ and $\alpha>0$. Let $\hat x$ be an **α-optimal** solution of PC (9): $\hat x\in D$ and
--
--   $$
--   \Gamma_s\Bigl(\sum_{i\in[n]}\hat x_iv_iv_i^\top\Bigr)\ge z^{LD}-\alpha ,
--   $$
--
--   where $z^{LD}$ is the value of PC (9). Let $\tilde S$ be the random size-$s$ subset with law (13), $\mathbb P[\tilde S=S]=\prod_{i\in S}\hat x_i\big/\sum_{\bar S\in\binom{[n]}{s}}\prod_{i\in\bar S}\hat x_i$. Then
--
--   $$
--   \log\mathbb E\Bigl[{\det}^s\Bigl(\sum_{i\in\tilde S}v_iv_i^\top\Bigr)\Bigr] \;\ge\; z^* - s\log s - \log\binom ns + s\log n - \alpha ,
--   $$
--
--   where $z^*$ is the MESP optimal value (1). Thus the sampling procedure is an $(s\log s+\log\binom ns-s\log n+\alpha)$-approximation for MESP in the log-determinant objective.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The expectation is the finite sum over the size-$s$ subsets weighted by the law (13). The left side and $z^*$ are extended reals with $\log0=-\infty$; logarithms are natural.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: the random set generated by the sampling Algorithm 2 → the random set with law (13), whose expectation is the finite sum sampleExpect V s x̂; reason: §3.2 defines the sampling procedure by (13); Algorithm 2 is its implementation, whose correctness the paper does not prove
--   - encoding: log E[…] and z* with log 0 = −∞ → EReal via ENNReal.log ∘ ENNReal.ofReal; real constants coerced; reason: Lean convention; same instances
--   - encoding: z* of MESP (2) → zStar V s, the value of MESP (1) with C = VᵀV; reason: (1) and (2) have the same value by Observation 1
--   - encoding: z^LD in the α-optimality condition (p.11) → zPC V s, the supremum of PC (9) in EReal; reason: the value of (9), attained by Theorem 2; x̂ feasible is x ∈ feasD n s
--   - encoding: C ⪰ 0 of rank d, C = VᵀV with V ∈ ℝ^{d×n}, and s ≤ d ≤ n → V : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin n) ℝ with V.rank = d and 1 ≤ s ≤ d; reason: C enters only through its factor V; d ≤ n follows from rank V = d ≤ n, so no instance changes
-- source:
--   Li, Xie, Best Principal Submatrix Selection for the Maximum Entropy Sampling Problem, arXiv:2001.08537v3, Theorem 5, p.14 (PDF p.14), proof p.15

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_PropVolSampling_Reduction_Model
import Definitions.Def_MaxEntSampling_GammaS
import Definitions.Def_MaxEntSampling_feasD
import Definitions.Def_MaxEntSampling_zStar
import Definitions.Def_MaxEntSampling_zPC
import Definitions.Def_MaxEntSampling_sampleExpect

namespace MaxEntSampling

open PropVolSampling.Reduction

/-- **Theorem 5: sampling from an α-optimal solution of PC (9) is within
`s log s + log C(n,s) − s log n + α` of the MESP optimum.**
Li, Xie, *Best Principal Submatrix Selection for the Maximum Entropy Sampling Problem*,
arXiv:2001.08537v3, Theorem 5, p.14 (PDF p.14), proof p.15: "Given an α-optimal solution x̂ of
PC (9) with α ∈ (0,∞), the random set generated by the sampling Algorithm 2 returns a
(s log(s) + log(C(n,s)) − s log(n) + α)-approximation bound for MESP (2), i.e., suppose the
output of Algorithm 2 is the random set S̃, then
log E[det^s(Σ_{i∈S̃} vi vi^⊤)] ≥ z∗ − s log(s) − log(C(n,s)) + s log(n) − α."

Let `V ∈ ℝ^{d×n}` have rank `d` (so `C = VᵀV ⪰ 0` has rank `d`), `s ∈ [d]` and `α > 0`. Let
`x̂ ∈ D` be α-optimal for PC (9), i.e. `Γ_s(Σ_i x̂_i v_i v_iᵀ) ≥ z^LD − α` with `z^LD` the value of
(9) (§3.1, p.11). Let `S̃` be the random size-`s` subset with law (13),
`P[S̃ = S] = ∏_{i∈S} x̂_i / Σ_{|S̄|=s} ∏_{i∈S̄} x̂_i`. Then
`log E[det^s(Σ_{i∈S̃} v_i v_iᵀ)] ≥ z* − s log s − log C(n,s) + s log n − α`.

**Formalization Note.** The random set "generated by the sampling Algorithm 2" is the random set
with law (13), and its expectation is the finite sum `sampleExpect V s x̂` (the first equality of
the proof, p.15). All logarithms are natural; the left side and `z*` lie in `EReal` with
`log 0 = −∞` (`ENNReal.log ∘ ENNReal.ofReal`), and the real constants are coerced. `z*` is the
MESP value (1) for `C = VᵀV`, equal to (2) by Observation 1. The hypothesis `d ≤ n` of the paper
follows from `rank V = d`. -/
theorem theorem_5 {d n : ℕ} (V : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin n) ℝ) (s : ℕ) (hs1 : 1 ≤ s) (hsd : s ≤ d)
    (hrank : V.rank = d) (α : ℝ) (hα : 0 < α) (x : Fin n → ℝ) (hx : x ∈ feasD n s)
    (hopt : zPC V s - (α : EReal) ≤ GammaS s (weightedGram V x)) :
    zStar V s - ((s * Real.log s : ℝ) : EReal) - ((Real.log (n.choose s : ℝ) : ℝ) : EReal) +
        ((s * Real.log n : ℝ) : EReal) - (α : EReal) ≤
      ENNReal.log (ENNReal.ofReal (sampleExpect V s x)) := by sorry

end MaxEntSampling
